{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAqdDPrLzVMr9YUfnMu9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found 17 problems a real MCP client would hit and wrote them up in `ANSWER.md`, ranked by severity, with a suggested fix order. I didn't change `server.py`. I ran it locally and sent it test requests with curl, and most findings are marked as reproduced against the running server; the rest come from reading the code.\n\n**The four that break or derail a client:**\n1. **HTTP 401 is used to refuse single calls.** Anonymous `add_note` and the `resources/*` methods return a 401. Clients read a 401 as \"this whole connection is unauthenticated\" and start a sign-in flow. That flow can't work: there's no `WWW-Authenticate` header and no OAuth discovery endpoint (every GET returns 405). The likely result is that clients mark the server as needing auth and the read tools stop working too. It's worse because `initialize` tells anonymous clients that resources are available, so they hit the 401 right at startup.\n2. **Bad arguments crash the handler and the client gets no response at all.** I reproduced four cases, including `search_notes` with `limit: null` (models often send `null` for optional fields), `add_note` without `body`, and a request body of `null`. The client just sees the connection drop, and the crash never reaches your JSON log.\n3. **Notifications other than `notifications/initialized` get an error reply with `id: null`.** That includes `notifications/cancelled`, which clients send when a call times out or the user interrupts. The spec says to reply `202` with no body.\n4. **The server doesn't check inputs against its own schema, so it can return output that breaks its declared schema.** `add_note` accepted an empty title. After that, `get_note` on that note returns data its `outputSchema` forbids, so clients that validate output will reject every read of that note.\n\n**Medium:**\n- **Tool failures are sent as protocol errors.** For example, \"note not found\" isn't returned as an `isError` result, so the model may never see the message and can't recover.\n- **The `Origin` header isn't checked.** Reads need no auth, so any web page the user visits could read the notes through a DNS-rebinding attack.\n- **`add_note` can hand out duplicate ids.** Two calls at the same moment can get the same id, and one note silently overwrites the other.\n- **Batch requests are still accepted,** even though the protocol version the server announces removed them.\n\nThe low-priority items include ignoring the client's requested protocol version, sessions that are issued but never checked, wrong `search_notes` snippet offsets, and the default dev token being published in the README.\n\nI stopped the server afterwards and removed my temporary files, so `ANSWER.md` is the only new file. The test note I created was only in memory and was lost when the server stopped; `notes.json` is unchanged."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3380,"cache_read_input_tokens":38601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3380},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66ab467b-561b-4af8-ac9f-69856b4395a9","uuid":"1884c962-1a39-421f-b5e2-a311f6a37ab7","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:53:04.162Z","request_id":"req_011CgAqdD6jXNA8PwVTXNBfa"}
